The AmigaOS layer of the owner's terminal programs on Zenix (roadmap block 3; DESIGN D27 section 9; Sumi, Maki,
Markdown and Ahiru, ports/sumi, ports/maki, ports/markdown, ports/ahiru; docs/d27-c-library.md, "Sumi on Zenix",
"Maki and Markdown on Zenix" and "Ahiru on Zenix")

What it is: the owner's programs call AmigaOS directly (they have no OS layer of their own), so their upstream files
compile unchanged over AmigaOS's names given here over the C library (GPL-2.0-only). One layer for all four, never
a copy each: dos.c is compiled once per program (build/PROG/zenix-dos.o) with the settings names that program maps
(ZENIX_TT_NAMES, the Makefile's TT_NAMES_PROG; Ahiru's in its own rules), and linked with the program's files, the
text-UI library's pure build (build/libtui-pure.a, ports/tui) and the C library's (build/libc-pure.a).

include/   amigados.h: the types, the structures upstream reads (FileInfoBlock, DateStamp, DateTime, FileHandle,
           DosPacket, the clipboard's request) and the calls, each one's meaning on Zenix in its head; the exec/, dos/,
           devices/, intuition/ and proto/ headers upstream includes are amigados.h (and so are those the text-UI
           library's headers include, in this build); proto/utf8.h (Maki's composing, never reached) and
           inline/macros.h (Markdown's call into openurl.library, never reached): no library opens on Zenix
dos.c      the calls: Open/Read/Write/Seek/Close/FGets (a handle is the descriptor + 1, so 0 stays "failed"; "*" the
           terminal, /dev/tty), Input/Output/IsInteractive, Lock/UnLock/Examine/ExNext/SameLock/CreateDir (a lock is
           the path, its device and inode), DeleteFile, Rename, SetProtection (the Unix mode, and root's owner),
           IoErr and Fault (errno and strerror), CompareDates, DateToStr, GetVar (getenv), Delay, SystemTags
           (system(), with SYS_Input and SYS_Output as the command's descriptors 0 and 1), FindTask, DOS packets
           (SendPkt/GetMsg: a pipe read when it has data), the reply ports' signal bits for the text-UI library's
           wait (tui_zenix_sigfd), the clipboard device (never opens), OpenLibrary (only "tui.library", the one
           linked in, opens); for Ahiru and the CFile core also (amigados.h's last part): AllocMem/FreeMem,
           AvailMem (SYS_meminfo), MODE_READWRITE, FPuts/FPutC, NameFromLock (soft links resolved), DupLock,
           ParentDir, CurrentDir (chdir), Info (SYS_statfs of the mount), DateStamp and SetFileDate (SYS_utimes),
           Execute (the shell; "" the user's), SystemTags' SYS_Asynch (the shell's &), Wait (the terminal's signal
           from the text-UI library and the reply ports' descriptors, by poll), SysBase->ThisTask
gui.c      Ahiru's screen calls (include/amigagui.h: Intuition, graphics, diskfont, icon): none ever opens; OpenFont
           gives a font all the same (upstream reads its size before it tries a screen)

Decided here (conservatively; for the main session to confirm):
- The names: only the program's own literals and a few AmigaOS places are mapped, nothing else is ever rewritten (a
  file the user names is a Unix path as typed): "ENVARC:NAME" is in $HOME/.config/name/ for the program's names
  (Sumi; Maki and FileColours; Markdown: TT_NAMES_PROG), lowercase, one folder a program, a file named for what it
  holds (docs/audit-2026-11-23-one-system.md OS-29): ENVARC:Sumi/Sumi.keys is $HOME/.config/sumi/keys, .prefs and
  .places likewise, ENVARC:ctop.keys $HOME/.config/ctop/keys, ENVARC:FileColours $HOME/.config/filecolours; the folder
  made at the first start, mode 700 (the places file lists the files read or edited). A file still at its old place
  (the AmigaOS names under $HOME/.config, before 2026-11-23) is moved to the new one the first time it is asked for,
  with a line on the error stream saying so, and its old folder goes when it is empty; "ENV:NAME" is no file (AmigaOS keeps a RAM copy of the settings there; on Zenix one place
  is enough, so that copy is never written and never read); "T:" (and so "T:PROG-") and "RAM:" are /tmp/T-UID, this user's own folder
  there (made with mode 700; one of that name that is not the user's own, or is a link, is refused: audit B3-7), "SYS:"
  /, "NIL:" /dev/null (the places Ahiru's literals and its default keys name; a Unix name starting so is rare: such a
  name in the current folder is taken for the place); "PROGDIR:NAME.EXT" of the program's own name is
  $HOME/.config/name/EXT (Ahiru keeps its files beside itself on AmigaOS: PROGDIR:Ahiru.keys is ahiru/keys; the
  folder is made, mode 700, when one is written). No HOME: no settings are kept.
- The command line (zmain.c, docs/audit-2026-11-23-one-system.md section 3): --help (the manual page's SYNOPSIS),
  --version and, for the programs with a keys file, -K (the built-in keys: upstream's own table, made global by the
  Makefile's objcopy as zport_keys) answered before upstream's main runs (-Wl,--wrap=main); upstream's source is
  unchanged.
- AmigaOS's meanings kept where Zenix's differ (audit 2026-10-30, block 3): Rename never replaces an existing other
  object (ERROR_OBJECT_EXISTS; Zenix's rename(2) would, and upstream relies on the refusal); MODE_OLDFILE opens for
  reading and writing (read only when writing is not allowed: Ahiru writes disk images in place); SameLock tells
  mounts apart by the mount table (Zenix's stat has no device); DeleteFile takes a folder on EISDIR or EPERM; ExNext
  lists a name whose path is over 255 bytes as an empty file nobody may read, so a copy fails on it instead of leaving
  it out; SystemTags and Execute turn upstream's AmigaDOS quotes ("NAME", where nothing is expanded) into sh's single
  quotes, so a name holding $, ` or \ is not run as a command (a name holding a " still ends its quotes early: upstream
  would need to pass the name apart from the command text).
- FGets keeps one read-ahead buffer for every handle: switching it between two handles drops what was read ahead
  from the first (no caller does; audit B3-24).
- Protection bits: FileInfoBlock's protection carries the Unix mode (bits 8-19, bit 31 marking it) and AmigaOS's r, w
  and e bits, set when this user may not read or write the file, or it has no x bit (upstream then shows [read-only]
  and refuses to save over it; Maki's listing shows rwed as AmigaOS's List does, and its colours take a file with x
  and no name ending for a program). FIBF_DELETE is never set: a directory that cannot be written fails the save with
  its own message. SetProtection puts the mode back after a save (a new copy renamed over the old file) and, when root
  saves, the owner and group Examine saw. Comments: Zenix files have none; Examine gives an empty one. A soft link is
  listed as what it points to (stat, not lstat: upstream would take ST_SOFTLINK's 3, above 0, for a directory).
- Dates: DateStamp counts days from 1978, as AmigaOS's does (AMIGA_EPOCH_DAYS): the CFile core turns them into
  dates itself and writes them into archives and disk images; Sumi, Maki and Markdown leave them to this layer
  (CompareDates, DateToStr in local time).
- Locks hold an absolute path, made from the current directory and freed of "." and ".." parts as written (as the
  shell's cd); NameFromLock resolves the soft links on the way (at most 8). IoErr gives AmigaOS's numbers for what
  upstream tests (not found, in use, exists, wrong type, across devices, not empty, disk full, write protected),
  Fault the text of either.
- Wait: the signals that can come are the text-UI library's (TuiSignal's bit: the terminal has input) and the
  reply ports' (their pipe has data); one asked with none of them behind it never comes, so a Wait for nothing
  else returns 0 at once.
- SystemTags with SYS_Asynch: the command through the shell's & (with /dev/null as its input and output unless
  handles are given), so it is nobody's child; "C:Ed " (Ahiru's default editor without $EDITOR) is Zenix's sumi,
  as "Sumi " is.
- "*" (AmigaOS's console of the program) is /dev/tty: Maki reading a pipe takes its keys from the terminal.
- Packets: upstream's ACTION_READ (Maki reading a pipe while it shows it) is a read done when the descriptor has data:
  SendPkt notes it on the reply port, GetMsg polls the descriptor (never waiting) and reads; the reply port's signal
  bit (16-31, as AmigaOS gives a program's own) names that descriptor to TuiWait (tui_zenix_sigfd, ports/tui), so the
  wait ends when the pipe has more. Other packets fail (DoPkt: ACTION_DISK_INFO asks the console whether it is CTerm;
  Zenix's is not). A read still out when Maki closes the pipe stays (upstream's PA_IGNORE): nothing writes into it.
- Ctrl-C: SetSignal never has it (in raw mode ^C is a key, which upstream takes as Esc in a prompt); a long search
  in Maki cannot be stopped by it.
- SystemTags: through sh (system()), in the terminal; upstream's default editor "Sumi" (Maki's v without $EDITOR) is
  run as Zenix's sumi.
- The stack: upstream swaps to a 32 KB stack of its own when the Shell gave less; on Zenix the program's stack is the
  loader's (D27 9 rule 5) and FindTask answers a task with enough, so upstream never swaps (each program's README has
  its measurement).
- For ctop (ports/ctop; amigados.h's last part): AvailMem(MEMF_TOTAL) is what the kernel's boot log says it had to
  give ("mem: N KB chip, M KB fast free"; 0 when the log no longer holds the line), SysBase->AttnFlags the log's CPU
  line; timer.device opens and ReadEClock counts the 100 Hz tick (rate 100: the E-clock's own count wraps every 101
  minutes); a Unix path with a colon after it ("/mnt/nas:", a mount named as a volume) is that path; BADDR of a value
  above the handles is that address (a BSTR's, a MKBADDR's); "ENVARC:NAME" written makes $HOME/.config (and the
  program's folder) first, as
  AmigaOS's ENVARC: is always there; OpenLibrary asks zenix_openlib, a program's own layer's hook, for a library it
  does not know (ctop's bsdsocket.library); SysBase has the TaskReady and TaskWait lists (empty, for ctop's layer);
  the volumes (LockDosList) and SetTaskPri are ctop's layer's.

Tests: each program's (tools/sumi-test.sh, tools/maki-test.sh, tools/markdown-test.sh, tools/ahiru-test.sh,
tools/ctop-test.sh, in make check); their logs are read by tools/screenlog.py.

If upstream starts to use an AmigaOS call amigados.h does not have, the build says so (an undeclared function); the
call is added here, once, for every program.
